1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:06,154
This video shows you what smeltr does when a destination is down,

2
00:00:06,154 --> 00:00:08,430
and when it refuses a record.

3
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:12,665
Stop Elasticsearch, and send a record while it's down.

4
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:25,236
Issues flags search-out: the destination is unreachable.

5
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:29,572
smeltr keeps the record and tries again.

6
00:00:29,572 --> 00:00:34,484
It moves the source's place in Kafka only after every destination has it,

7
00:00:34,484 --> 00:00:36,430
so the records wait there.

8
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:39,251
Start Elasticsearch again.

9
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:44,134
The issue clears, and the record is in the index.

10
00:00:44,134 --> 00:00:45,960
Nothing was lost.

11
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:57,567
Now send a record Elasticsearch can't take.

12
00:00:57,567 --> 00:01:02,030
Its durationMs is text, and the index holds numbers there.

13
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:03,843
Elasticsearch refuses it.

14
00:01:03,843 --> 00:01:09,131
The default answer to a refusal is block: smeltr doesn't accept the record,

15
00:01:09,131 --> 00:01:12,710
so Kafka keeps it, and the source waits for you.

16
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:18,193
To see what was refused, open the Map, select search-out, and start a capture.

17
00:01:18,193 --> 00:01:23,112
It lists each record the destination refused, with the reason.

18
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:32,084
You can send refused records to a dead-letter destination,

19
00:01:32,084 --> 00:01:35,038
such as a Kafka topic, and look at them later.

20
00:01:35,038 --> 00:01:37,606
Or fix the pipeline, so the record fits.

21
00:01:37,606 --> 00:01:38,590
Do that now.

22
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:42,227
Open the pipeline, tutorial-in-parse.

23
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:51,680
Kafka still holds the blocked record, so capture it from the source, tutorial-in.

24
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:56,515
Start at the earliest record, and use the records as the sample.

25
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:08,757
In the Eval step, add one more assignment: durationMs as f64.

26
00:02:08,757 --> 00:02:11,926
It makes the field a number.

27
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:20,546
The text slow isn't a number, so durationMs becomes empty,

28
00:02:20,546 --> 00:02:26,190
and the record carries a CoercionRefused exception, so you can still find it.

29
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:31,772
Save it on a branch called deliver-duration, and open it for review.

30
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:44,996
The review shows one change, to tutorial-in-parse.

31
00:02:44,996 --> 00:02:47,190
Approve and deploy it.

32
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:50,408
Each worker stops reading, takes the new pipeline,

33
00:02:50,408 --> 00:02:53,957
and starts again from its last committed place in Kafka.

34
00:02:53,957 --> 00:02:56,310
The refused record is still there.

35
00:02:56,360 --> 00:03:01,630
So it's read again, through the fixed pipeline, and reaches Elasticsearch.

36
00:03:01,630 --> 00:03:07,072
Its exception says what was wrong, and _raw keeps the original text.

37
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:11,411
Check on learning.

38
00:03:11,611 --> 00:03:13,620
Elasticsearch was down.

39
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:16,055
Why was nothing lost?

40
00:03:20,455 --> 00:03:26,173
smeltr moves the source's place in Kafka only after every destination has the record,

41
00:03:26,173 --> 00:03:28,657
so the records waited there.

42
00:03:29,557 --> 00:03:32,980
Elasticsearch refused a record, and the source blocked.

43
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:36,692
Where was the record while you fixed the pipeline?

44
00:03:41,092 --> 00:03:42,146
Still in Kafka.

45
00:03:42,146 --> 00:03:43,974
smeltr hadn't accepted it,

46
00:03:43,974 --> 00:03:49,213
so after the deploy it was read again, through the fixed pipeline.

47
00:03:50,113 --> 00:03:52,066
That's Deliver to your stack.

48
00:03:52,066 --> 00:03:55,838
You sent records to Elasticsearch, S3 and OpenTelemetry,

49
00:03:55,838 --> 00:04:00,547
and kept them safe when a destination failed or refused them.
